1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Welcome to School Physics, Explained.

2
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:08,237
This is High School Physics, Unit 1,
Measurement and Motion Basics, Part 1. In

3
00:00:08,237 --> 00:00:10,797
this video, we'll cover four topics.

4
00:00:11,247 --> 00:00:14,169
Why Physics? Models, Measurement and SI
Units.

5
00:00:14,619 --> 00:00:18,929
Scientific Notation, Significant Figures
and Unit Conversion.

6
00:00:19,379 --> 00:00:22,621
Scalars versus Vectors: Distance and
Displacement.

7
00:00:23,071 --> 00:00:24,458
Speed and Velocity.

8
00:00:24,908 --> 00:00:26,124
Let's get started.

9
00:00:27,766 --> 00:00:32,923
Why does a ball fall? How fast does light
travel? How much energy does a phone

10
00:00:32,923 --> 00:00:33,782
battery hold?

11
00:00:34,232 --> 00:00:38,791
Physics is the science that answers
questions like these, using careful

12
00:00:38,791 --> 00:00:40,717
measurement and simple models.

13
00:00:41,867 --> 00:00:46,561
A model is a simplified picture of reality
that we can calculate with.

14
00:00:47,011 --> 00:00:51,896
For a thrown ball, we might ignore the
air, and treat the ball as a single point.

15
00:00:52,346 --> 00:00:56,143
The model isn't perfect, but it predicts
the ball's path very well.

16
00:00:57,293 --> 00:01:00,472
Every measurement has two parts: a number,
and a unit.

17
00:01:00,922 --> 00:01:03,546
Saying a table is two long tells us
nothing.

18
00:01:03,996 --> 00:01:08,284
Two meters, two feet, and two centimeters
are very different tables.

19
00:01:09,434 --> 00:01:13,679
Scientists everywhere use the same system
of units, called S I.

20
00:01:14,129 --> 00:01:18,878
The three you'll use most are the meter
for length, the kilogram for mass, and the

21
00:01:18,878 --> 00:01:19,804
second for time.

22
00:01:20,254 --> 00:01:25,168
Other units are built from these. For
example, speed is measured in meters per

23
00:01:25,168 --> 00:01:25,609
second.

24
00:01:26,759 --> 00:01:31,004
For very large or very small amounts, S I
uses prefixes.

25
00:01:31,454 --> 00:01:35,763
Kilo means one thousand, so a kilometer is
one thousand meters.

26
00:01:36,213 --> 00:01:39,691
Centi means one hundredth, and milli means
one thousandth.

27
00:01:40,841 --> 00:01:44,062
Let's convert two point five kilometers to
meters.

28
00:01:44,512 --> 00:01:50,047
One kilometer is one thousand meters, so
we multiply two point five by one

29
00:01:50,047 --> 00:01:50,720
thousand.

30
00:01:51,170 --> 00:01:53,687
That gives two thousand five hundred
meters.

31
00:01:54,837 --> 00:01:56,309
Now try one yourself.

32
00:01:56,759 --> 00:02:01,538
A student sits three hundred fifty
centimeters from the board. How far is

33
00:02:01,538 --> 00:02:02,519
that in meters?

34
00:02:02,969 --> 00:02:04,249
Pause the video now.

35
00:02:04,699 --> 00:02:10,695
One meter is one hundred centimeters, so
divide by one hundred. The answer is three

36
00:02:10,695 --> 00:02:11,995
point five meters.

37
00:02:12,296 --> 00:02:17,011
The distance from Earth to the Sun is
about one hundred fifty billion meters.

38
00:02:17,461 --> 00:02:21,087
A human hair is about seventy millionths
of a meter wide.

39
00:02:21,537 --> 00:02:27,565
Writing all those zeros is slow, and easy
to get wrong. Scientific notation fixes

40
00:02:27,565 --> 00:02:27,937
that.

41
00:02:29,087 --> 00:02:34,970
In scientific notation, we write a number
as a value from one to ten, times a power

42
00:02:34,970 --> 00:02:35,466
of ten.

43
00:02:35,916 --> 00:02:41,493
One hundred fifty billion meters becomes
one point five times ten to the eleventh

44
00:02:41,493 --> 00:02:41,975
meters.

45
00:02:42,425 --> 00:02:47,545
Seventy millionths of a meter becomes
seven times ten to the minus five meters.

46
00:02:48,695 --> 00:02:51,553
Every measurement also has limited
precision.

47
00:02:52,003 --> 00:02:57,159
With a ruler marked in centimeters, we
might read a pencil's length as fourteen

48
00:02:57,159 --> 00:02:58,595
point six centimeters.

49
00:02:59,045 --> 00:03:02,779
The one and the four are certain, and the
six is our best estimate.

50
00:03:03,229 --> 00:03:08,251
The digits we can trust are called
significant figures. This measurement has

51
00:03:08,251 --> 00:03:08,647
three.

52
00:03:09,797 --> 00:03:15,128
When you multiply or divide, round the
answer to the fewest significant figures

53
00:03:15,128 --> 00:03:16,005
in your data.

54
00:03:16,455 --> 00:03:22,661
For example, two point five meters times
three point four two meters is eight point

55
00:03:22,661 --> 00:03:24,455
five five square meters.

56
00:03:24,905 --> 00:03:29,944
But two point five has only two
significant figures, so we report eight

57
00:03:29,944 --> 00:03:31,647
point six square meters.

58
00:03:32,797 --> 00:03:38,578
To convert units, multiply by conversion
factors: fractions that are equal to one.

59
00:03:39,028 --> 00:03:43,423
Let's convert seventy two kilometers per
hour into meters per second.

60
00:03:43,873 --> 00:03:49,269
Multiply by one thousand meters over one
kilometer, and by one hour over three

61
00:03:49,269 --> 00:03:51,275
thousand six hundred seconds.

62
00:03:51,725 --> 00:03:56,141
The kilometers and the hours cancel,
leaving twenty meters per second.

63
00:03:57,291 --> 00:03:58,763
Now try one yourself.

64
00:03:59,213 --> 00:04:03,096
Convert fifteen meters per second into
kilometers per hour.

65
00:04:03,546 --> 00:04:04,826
Pause the video now.

66
00:04:05,276 --> 00:04:10,401
Multiply by three thousand six hundred
seconds per hour, and divide by one

67
00:04:10,401 --> 00:04:15,665
thousand meters per kilometer. The answer
is fifty four kilometers per hour.

68
00:04:15,965 --> 00:04:18,909
You walk four meters east, then three
meters north.

69
00:04:19,359 --> 00:04:23,370
How far did you go? The answer depends on
what we mean by far.

70
00:04:23,820 --> 00:04:27,724
Physics has two different answers, called
distance and displacement.

71
00:04:28,874 --> 00:04:34,091
Some quantities only need a size. Mass,
time and temperature are like this. We

72
00:04:34,091 --> 00:04:35,295
call them scalars.

73
00:04:35,745 --> 00:04:40,294
Other quantities need a size and a
direction. Pushes, velocities and

74
00:04:40,294 --> 00:04:43,638
displacements are like this. We call them
vectors.

75
00:04:44,088 --> 00:04:48,790
We draw a vector as an arrow. Its length
shows the size, and it points in the

76
00:04:48,790 --> 00:04:49,400
direction.

77
00:04:50,550 --> 00:04:55,414
Distance is a scalar. It's the total
length of the path you actually traveled.

78
00:04:55,864 --> 00:05:00,174
In our walk, that's four meters plus three
meters, which is seven meters.

79
00:05:01,324 --> 00:05:06,289
Displacement is a vector. It's a straight
arrow from where you started to where you

80
00:05:06,289 --> 00:05:06,828
ended up.

81
00:05:07,278 --> 00:05:12,225
Here it forms the long side of a right
triangle, so we use the Pythagorean

82
00:05:12,225 --> 00:05:12,760
theorem.

83
00:05:13,210 --> 00:05:18,245
Three squared plus four squared is twenty
five, and the square root of twenty five

84
00:05:18,245 --> 00:05:18,736
is five.

85
00:05:19,186 --> 00:05:22,748
So the displacement is five meters,
pointing north of east.

86
00:05:23,898 --> 00:05:27,632
Distance can never be smaller than the
size of the displacement.

87
00:05:28,082 --> 00:05:32,992
Run one full lap around a four hundred
meter track, and your distance is four

88
00:05:32,992 --> 00:05:33,948
hundred meters.

89
00:05:34,398 --> 00:05:37,897
But you end where you started, so your
displacement is zero.

90
00:05:39,047 --> 00:05:40,519
Now try one yourself.

91
00:05:40,969 --> 00:05:46,006
A dog runs ten meters east, then comes
back six meters west. What is its

92
00:05:46,006 --> 00:05:48,734
distance, and what is its displacement?

93
00:05:49,184 --> 00:05:50,464
Pause the video now.

94
00:05:50,914 --> 00:05:56,254
The distance is ten plus six, which is
sixteen meters. The displacement is ten

95
00:05:56,254 --> 00:05:58,786
minus six, which is four meters east.

96
00:05:59,086 --> 00:06:02,393
A car's speedometer reads sixty kilometers
per hour.

97
00:06:02,843 --> 00:06:05,061
But is the car heading north, or south?

98
00:06:05,511 --> 00:06:08,306
Speed and velocity answer different
questions.

99
00:06:09,456 --> 00:06:14,320
Average speed is the total distance
traveled, divided by the time it took.

100
00:06:14,770 --> 00:06:18,034
A runner covers four hundred meters in
eighty seconds.

101
00:06:18,484 --> 00:06:24,052
The runner's average speed is four hundred
divided by eighty, which is five meters

102
00:06:24,052 --> 00:06:24,799
per second.

103
00:06:25,949 --> 00:06:29,853
Average velocity is the displacement,
divided by the time.

104
00:06:30,303 --> 00:06:35,783
Because displacement is a vector, velocity
is a vector too. It has a size, and a

105
00:06:35,783 --> 00:06:36,468
direction.

106
00:06:37,618 --> 00:06:41,970
Back to the runner. Those four hundred
meters were one full lap of the track.

107
00:06:42,420 --> 00:06:46,217
The runner ends where they started, so the
displacement is zero.

108
00:06:46,667 --> 00:06:52,205
That makes the average velocity zero, even
though the average speed was five meters

109
00:06:52,205 --> 00:06:52,939
per second.

110
00:06:54,089 --> 00:06:59,081
Instantaneous speed is how fast you're
going at a single moment. That's what a

111
00:06:59,081 --> 00:07:00,233
speedometer shows.

112
00:07:00,683 --> 00:07:04,139
Add a direction, and you have the
instantaneous velocity.

113
00:07:04,589 --> 00:07:09,048
If either the speed or the direction
changes, the velocity changes.

114
00:07:10,198 --> 00:07:11,670
Now try one yourself.

115
00:07:12,120 --> 00:07:17,717
A cyclist rides twelve hundred meters
north in four minutes. What is the average

116
00:07:17,717 --> 00:07:19,885
velocity, in meters per second?

117
00:07:20,335 --> 00:07:21,615
Pause the video now.

118
00:07:22,065 --> 00:07:27,747
Four minutes is two hundred forty seconds.
Twelve hundred divided by two hundred

119
00:07:27,747 --> 00:07:32,860
forty is five. So the average velocity is
five meters per second, north.

120
00:07:33,310 --> 00:07:37,939
Next time, we'll look at how velocity
changes. That's acceleration.

121
00:07:39,089 --> 00:07:43,924
If this lesson helped, subscribe to School
Physics, Explained, and tap the bell so

122
00:07:43,924 --> 00:07:45,575
you don't miss the next one.

